**Read:** [Inorganic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Inorganic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_77Z8T5RFYFNNXZ7XKZFKPRKDKR), the complete industrial-gases production and property sequence; [Industrial Chemical Technology II — Nduka, NOUN](../sources/Industrial-Chemical-Technology-II-NOUN.lingua#r_7RXE2GF1DW7QH9GHTCGT0P09SR), the complete gas/feed and technical-process context; [Graduate Chemical Engineering Thermodynamics — MIT 10.40](../sources/Graduate-Thermodynamics-MIT-10-40.lingua#r_62DYAQ1KKK4AWYQMYAEN964VJT), the full mixing, separation-work and phase-model derivations.

Air supplies chemically useful gases, but their required purity, pressure and physical form depend on the application. A bulk oxygen supply, an inerting gas and a sensitive process atmosphere need different impurity and delivery evidence. Trace water, carbon dioxide, oxygen or particles can matter even when the major-component fraction is high.

Compare cryogenic, adsorption and membrane routes through their actual phase/transport principles, recovery and product requirements. Feed conditioning, compression, cooling, regeneration and returned streams are part of the process. A nominal gas percentage cannot determine the energy account or interchangeable use.

For an ideal binary feed with mole fractions 0.79 and 0.21, the reversible isothermal work of complete unmixing at temperature T is −RTΣz_i ln z_i per mole of feed. This is a defined thermodynamic bound, not the power consumption of an actual air-separation unit, which also has different products, recovery, pressure and irreversibilities.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete public gas-production route from conditioned feed to specified delivered product. Compare two technologies at equal useful output and pressure/purity requirements. Close both gas and utility accounts, identify influential contaminants and explain the measurement/support needed to test a claimed performance improvement.

